    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d584">

                <pb facs="0739" n="625" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg625"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.3911/orig.: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Commissioner in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-04-01T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 1, 1924—5
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>10. Your 25, March 27, 3 p.m. Since no provision therefor exists it is not the
                    practice of the Federal Government to invite Chiefs of States or
                    Presidents-elect to visit the United States as the guests of the Government; but
                    you may say to the President-elect that should he desire to come to the United
                    States and to visit Washington, the President will be most happy to receive
                    him.</p>
                <p>See confidential circular February 28, 1903,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d584-f1"/> to
                    diplomatic officers, in Legation’s files.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d584-f1" n="3" xml:id="d584-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in it diplomatic officers were informed that neither American law
                    nor usages provided for inviting foreign sovereigns and chiefs of state to visit
                    the United States.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
